---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-in-pregnancy`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D in Pregnancy: Requirements, Risks, and Evidence',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: [
    { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
    { '@type': 'PeopleAudience', suggestedGender: 'female' },
  ],
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D in Pregnancy', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D in Pregnancy: Dosage, Risks & Evidence"
  description="What pregnant women need to know about vitamin D â€” RDA, deficiency risks (preeclampsia, gestational diabetes, low birth weight), safe supplementation, and 25(OH)D targets."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-in-pregnancy", label: "Pregnancy" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />


  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D in Pregnancy</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Adequate maternal 25-hydroxyvitamin D is important for fetal skeletal development and appears
      to reduce the risk of several pregnancy complications. Deficiency is common â€” surveys show
      30â€“60% of pregnant women in temperate countries have serum 25(OH)D below 30 ng/mL.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Recommended intake</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      The IOM sets the RDA in pregnancy at <strong>600 IU/day (15 Âµg)</strong>, the same as
      non-pregnant adults. The Endocrine Society recommends up to <strong>1,500â€“2,000 IU/day</strong>
      to maintain serum 25(OH)D at â‰¥ 40 ng/mL, arguing this level is more appropriate for maternal
      and fetal health. The upper limit remains 4,000 IU/day.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deficiency-associated risks</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      <li><strong>Preeclampsia</strong>: meta-analyses show a 40â€“50% relative-risk reduction with supplementation, especially when started early.</li>
      <li><strong>Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM)</strong>: deficient women have ~1.5Ã— the odds; supplementation modestly reduces incidence.</li>
      <li><strong>Small-for-gestational-age (SGA) births</strong>: reduced with supplementation in deficient populations.</li>
      <li><strong>Neonatal hypocalcaemia</strong>: severe maternal deficiency can lead to hypocalcaemic seizures in the newborn.</li>
      <li><strong>Craniotabes and congenital rickets</strong>: rare but reported in infants of severely deficient mothers.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Testing during pregnancy</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Serum 25(OH)D testing is recommended at the first prenatal visit for women with
      risk factors: darker skin, veiling, obesity, malabsorption, or a history of gestational
      diabetes. Below 20 ng/mL, most obstetrics guidelines advise 2,000â€“4,000 IU/day cholecalciferol
      through pregnancy and lactation. Confirm dosing with your obstetrician.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">After birth: mother and baby</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Breast milk contains very little vitamin D. All breastfed infants should receive
      <strong>400 IU/day cholecalciferol</strong> from birth (AAP 2008; Health Canada). Alternatively,
      maternal supplementation with 6,400 IU/day has been shown to raise breast-milk vitamin D
      to levels sufficient for the infant (Hollis et al. 2015), though this dose exceeds the
      standard UL and requires physician oversight.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Pregnancy vitamin D management should be individualised â€”
      consult your obstetrician or midwife. Sarcoidosis, hyperparathyroidism, and other conditions
      that predispose to hypercalcaemia contraindicate high-dose vitamin D.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-in-pregnancy" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
